      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The buffering capacity of your dough is essentially the chemical \"shield\" your flour provides against those sudden pH drops, and it is heavily dependent on the specific proteins and minerals within your flour blend. When you use a high-protein bread flour, you are introducing more glutenin and gliadin, which form a robust gluten network capable of trapping more water and creating a larger surface area for chemical reactions. This dense network acts like a thick goblin's cloak, slowing down the rate at which acidic byproducts can penetrate and destabilize the dough structure.\n\nHowever, the type of flour matters significantly more than just the protein percentage. Rye flour, for instance, is rich in pentosans (a type of fiber) and contains different mineral profiles, particularly higher levels of phosphates and magnesium. These minerals act as natural buffers, similar to how a goblin might carry a heavy shield to block attacks, but in this case, they chemically neutralize the excess acid produced by the lactobacilli before it can lower the pH too drastically. This is why rye starters often maintain a more stable acidity even in fluctuating temperatures compared to wheat-based starters.\n\nIf your kitchen temperature swings are causing those rapid pH drops, switching to a flour",
